this is mount kilauea  hawaii's most active volcano  three years ago scientists were taken by  surprise  when lava began spewing out from cracks  in the lower slopes of kilauea  50 kilometers from the volcano's summit  the rate of should produce new lava was  almost unprecedented in historic times  so it wasn't so much the sheer size of  it it covered a very small part of the  state it was the speed with which it  didn't  in total one cubic kilometer of lava  flowed out over three months  rivers of runny lava ran down to the  ocean destroying hundreds of homes  volcanologists like bruce hauken and  matthew patrick  have been studying kilauea for decades  this is the best monitored volcano in  the world  so matthew probably wouldn't say that to  you because  yeah it would it would be a boast but  it's it's not it's the most  intensely balanced volcano and then it  had a very intense eruption  this makes kilauea the perfect volcanic  lab for studying how  eruptions happen since the 2018 eruption  the team have been piecing together the  events that triggered it  so that next time a volcano of this type  erupts  we can be better prepared  kilauea is a basaltic volcano which  means it has very fluid magma below the  surface  and tends to leak runny lava rather than  erupting explosively  at the summit there's a crater known as  hale maumau  lava has been bubbling to the surface of  halemaumau  since 2008 forming this lava lake  nearby is another crater called puo  lava has been slowly flowing out of pua  and down to the ocean  almost continuously since 1983.  to the east of puoa is an area called  the lower east rift zone  the last eruption in this zone was in  1961  after that it became a popular place to  live partly because the land here is  cheap compared with the rest of the  island but this  is where the lava flowed out in 2018  taking residents and researchers by  surprise  we had seen the pressure building at  pual and summit  and we were expecting a relatively small  change at the pua  eruption site but in 2018  for reasons that i think we still don't  fully understand that pressurization  triggered a bigger change a deeper  change  that presumably cleared a blockage that  had been restricting maglin moving  east of pua on april 30th we started to  see earthquakes  migrate east down rift so those  earthquakes migrating down rift  were a clear sign that magma was  intruding through the volcano farther  down the rift zone  it was a clear indication that something  new  and something potentially very hazardous  was was happening  on the third of may the first cracks or  fishes  appeared in the lower east rift zone  bruce rushed over there  the notion that something driven by  processes  under the summer of the volcano is going  to create something  50 kilometers away is hard to encompass  but once the fishes first formed i i was  confident that they were going to  propagate outwards you know  that you would open up one then another  then another  and what i really wanted to do was to be  around  for as many of the fish's opening as  possible  bruce used high-speed cameras to film  the gas and particles that were spat  out from the fishes first before the  lava flows got going  back in the lab by slowing the footage  down we get the size of the particles  the shape of the particles  we also can measure exactly how much is  being erupted per unit time so  the estimates of intensity come from  from those videos as well  we just measure the total amount of  material that's in the air at any second  another team analyzed the chemistry of  the lava itself  and realized that in those first weeks  it was cooler  stickier old magma coming out once that  was flushed through  they predicted that hotter more fluid  magma would arrive  from the summit magma chamber and  i remember being on one of the first  overflights after that kind of  change happened in the eruption and i  had never seen  so much lava on the surface and once we  started to see the arrival of this hot  fluid magma the hazard of the eruption  totally changed  and unfortunately it became much more  destructive  the lava covered farmland roads and  around  700 houses reaching the ocean in early  june  meanwhile at the volcano's summit  another historic event was unfolding  the crater was collapsing as magma from  the reservoir  underneath it drained away to the  eruption site  there have only been a handful of  caldera collapses that have been  monitored with  modern geophysical instrumentation what  we saw  is that the caldera collapse was not  just one big event  basically every day or so we saw the  crater floor drop several meters each  time the crater floor dropped  it caused an earthquake with a magnitude  of about 5.3  it also briefly pressurized the magma  chamber  and that sent out a pressure pulse down  towards the lower east rift zone  which caused a surge in the lava that  was flowing out there  it was really remarkable to see the  changes happening at the summit  then being transmitted basically within  minutes to produce these surges and lava  eruption rates down on the lower flank  that had direct implications for hazard  i think the biggest thing that we've  learned is  just how connected all the parts of the  system  are so a lot of people now are revising  their models for how  how you get molten rock to move not not  simply vertically  under the volcano but how you get it to  move 50 kilometers  relatively rapidly and reappear  somewhere else  um because that's the part that we have  least understanding of because it's the  part we can't see  at the moment volcanology is all about  basically simple pattern recognition  it's not about understanding  the processes the mathematics or the  physics of what's going on and that's  what we're trying to transform into
 

